## Review Complete

**Verdict: The constant is correct within f64 precision.**

### Arithmetic verification

| Value | Result |
|---|---|
| NIST BTU_IT | 1055.05585262 J |
| 1 therm (100,000 BTU) | 105,505,585.262 J |
| 1 therm/hour ÷ 3600 | **29,307.107017222...** W (repeating 2) |
| HARES constant | 29,307.1070172222 W (truncated) |
| Error in f64 | -2.18 × 10⁻¹¹ W (≈ 0.74 ULP) |

The truncation after 10 decimal digits is **sub-ULP** in f64 — the stored binary representations of both values are identical. No simulation result is affected.

Two **low-severity** findings written to `docs/reviews/physics-constants/phys-const-05-gas-therm-hour-to-watt.md`:
1. The constant truncates the exact repeating decimal (benign, sub-ULP)
2. The "Consistent with pint UnitRegistry" comment could be clarified since pint's BTU definition chain may differ slightly from NIST SP 811 depending on pint version
